---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Case Studies | Prodory" description="How AO+ runs its own Kubernetes stack on three nodes: WordPress, ERPNext and Grafana monitoring on one platform." path="/case-studies/">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[900px] px-4 py-16">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[#1D4ED8]">Case study</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight">Running AO+ on a three-node Kubernetes stack</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-[#4B5563]">AO+ runs its own business applications on a three-node Kubernetes cluster: WordPress for the websites, ERPNext for accounts and operations, and Grafana for monitoring. The same platform practice we offer clients is used on our own systems first.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">The setup</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 grid list-disc gap-3 pl-5 text-[#4B5563]">
      <li>Three Kubernetes nodes running one cluster.</li>
      <li>WordPress for the public websites.</li>
      <li>ERPNext for accounting, stock and operations.</li>
      <li>Grafana dashboards for monitoring the cluster and the applications on it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold">Why it matters for clients</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[#4B5563]">Three different application types share one cluster and one monitoring view. That is the same pattern we set up for clients who want a small, operable platform rather than a collection of servers.</p>

    <p class="mt-12 rounded border border-[#E5E7EB] bg-[#F8F9FA] p-5 text-sm text-[#4B5563]">We do not publish performance or cost figures for this stack until they have been measured and checked. If you would like to discuss a similar setup, <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/architecture-review/">book an architecture review</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
